Key points on why this is a true sliding window:
- Per-client state is an array of actual request timestamps, not a counter tied to a fixed interval.
- On every request, timestamps older than `now - windowMs` are pruned before counting, so the "window" is always `[now - windowMs, now]` — it moves continuously with each request rather than resetting at fixed clock boundaries.
- The 429 response's `Retry-After` is computed from when the oldest timestamp in the window will expire (`oldest + windowMs - now`), giving an accurate wait time rather than "time until next fixed interval."
- Memory is bounded by the periodic cleanup (default every 60s) that removes clients with no activity in the last `windowMs`, and `close()` clears the interval to avoid keeping the event loop alive (also set via `unref()` as a belt-and-suspenders measure).

